\description{
These functions play models of opinion dynamics upon networks,
in which nodes change their opinions towards those of the nodes
they listen to.
\itemize{
\item \code{play_opinion()} plays an opinion model upon a network,
and returns the network with a record of the opinions in its changes.
\item \code{as_opinion()} reads the record of an opinion from a network,
such as one that \code{play_opinion()} returns,
and returns a table of the opinion of each node at each step.
This table is of the class 'opinionModel',
with 'learn_model' as its second class for one release.
}

The opinions are numbers, such as the probability that a node
puts on some statement being true, or a position on a scale.
For opinions that are categories, see \code{\link[=play_diffusion]{play_diffusion()}}.
}
\section{Rules}{

\code{rule} chooses how the nodes update their opinions at each step:
\itemize{
\item \code{"degroot"} (the default): all nodes at once take the average of the
opinions of the nodes they listen to, weighted by the influence matrix W
(DeGroot 1974).
This is deterministic.
On a connected network with no stubbornness, it reaches a consensus.
\item \code{"hegselmann_krause"}: all nodes at once take the weighted average of
the opinions of only those nodes they listen to whose opinions are
within \code{confidence} of their own (Hegselmann and Krause 2002).
This is deterministic, and opinions can settle into several clusters.
\item \code{"deffuant"}: at each step, one node is drawn at random,
and it draws one of the nodes it listens to, in proportion to W.
If their opinions are within \code{confidence} of each other,
each moves halfway towards the other, where it listens to the other
(Deffuant et al. 2000).
This is stochastic, so set a seed to repeat a result.
Since only one pair can change at each step, this rule usually needs
many more \code{steps} than the others.
}

\code{confidence} is used only by the bounded-confidence rules,
\code{"hegselmann_krause"} and \code{"deffuant"}.
Where \code{opinions} hold several issues,
the distance between two nodes' opinions is the Euclidean distance
across the issues.
}

\section{Self-weight and stubbornness}{

Two quantities decide how much a node holds on to its own opinion:
\itemize{
\item Its \strong{self-weight} is how much it listens to itself,
compared with the nodes it listens to.
It is the diagonal of W, and in a network it is a self-loop:
a loop of weight 2 on a node with one tie of weight 1 to another node
gives that node a self-weight of 2/3 (see \code{\link[=to_influence]{to_influence()}}).
A self-weight pulls a node towards its \emph{current} opinion.
\item Its \strong{stubbornness} is how much it keeps its \emph{first} opinion,
compared with what the network says.
It is \code{stubbornness}, where 0 is DeGroot's model and
1 is a node that never moves from its first opinion.
}

The update is \eqn{x(t+1) = (I - S) W x(t) + S x(0)},
where \eqn{S} is the diagonal matrix of stubbornness.
This is the model of Friedkin and Johnsen (1990),
and \code{stubbornness} is 1 minus what they call susceptibility.

On a path A–B–C with the first opinions 0, 0.5, and 1:\tabular{lll}{
   Setting \tab Final opinions \tab What it shows \cr
   No self-weight, stubbornness 0 (DeGroot) \tab 0.5, 0.5, 0.5 \tab Consensus. \cr
   Self-weight 1/2 at every node \tab 0.5, 0.5, 0.5 \tab Slower, but the same consensus. \cr
   Self-weight 2/3 at A only \tab 0.33, 0.33, 0.33 \tab A has more influence, but there is still a consensus. \cr
   Stubbornness 0.5 at every node \tab 0.25, 0.5, 0.75 \tab Disagreement lasts. \cr
   Stubbornness 1 at A \tab 0, 0, 0 \tab A pulls everyone to its first opinion. \cr
   Self-weight 1 at A \tab 0, 0, 0 \tab The same as stubbornness 1: A listens only to itself. \cr
}


So a node that listens only to itself is fully stubborn.
That is the only case where the two quantities give the same result.
With self-weights alone, disagreement lasts only where two or more nodes
are fully stubborn, or where the network is not connected.
With \code{stubbornness}, disagreement lasts also where every node is only
partly stubborn.
}

\section{The record}{

\code{play_opinion()} returns the network it was given,
with the opinions at the start as a node attribute,
and each later change in the changes of the network.
A node gets a row for a step only where its opinion has moved by more
than \code{tolerance} since its last row, so the record stays small,
and \code{as_opinion()} rebuilds each step to within \code{tolerance}.
The run stops when no opinion moves by \code{tolerance} or more in a step,
or, for \code{"deffuant"}, when no pair that listens to each other
can move any more, or after \code{steps} steps.
Where the attribute named by \code{opinions} already has a record,
the run continues from the last opinions and the last time of that record.
}

